; ======================================================================
;  entropy.pi4 - THE BCM2711 HARDWARE RANDOM NUMBER GENERATOR (RNG200)
; ======================================================================
;
;      #SOC_TARGET = 2711                       ; only if soc.pi4 is used
;      XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/entropy.pi4"
;
;      Procedure Main()
;        Define st.i
;        Define n.i
;        Global Dim seed.a[48]
;        st = EntropyBegin()
;        If st < 0
;          ; refuse to continue - see THE REFUSALS below
;        EndIf
;        n = EntropyBytes(@seed[0], 48, #ENTROPY_SPINS)
;        ; n = 48 on success, negative on refusal
;      EndProcedure
;
;  This is the file drbg.pi4's header spent seventy lines asking for. It
;  enables the BCM2711's RNG200 block, waits out the warm-up the block
;  documents, reads its FIFO only when the block says the FIFO has
;  something in it, and offers a dead-block detector that is honest
;  about being a dead-block detector and nothing more.
;
;  ---- DEPENDENCIES ---------------------------------------------------
;
;  NONE. This file includes no other library (house rule: a library
;  never includes a library - the MAIN file lists what it needs). It
;  uses PeekN/PokeN, integer arithmetic, and the a64_barrier()
;  intrinsic from RaspberryPi4/Intrinsics/bcm2711_hardware.def.
;
;  It does NOT use timer.pi4, and that is a decision rather than an
;  omission - see THE WAITS ARE COUNTED IN SPINS below. Nor bits.pi4,
;  whose popcount this file would otherwise want - see the note above
;  entropy_Popcount32.
;
;  NO SYMBOL HERE COLLIDES with anything else in RaspberryPi4/Lib, and
;  that is a BUILD rather than an inspection: checked 2026-08-28 by
;  compiling one main that includes soc, bits, memory, timer, uart,
;  sort, math, string, string_f32, sha256, hmac, entropy and drbg
;  together and calls into this file.
;
;  ======================================================================
;   WHERE THE BLOCK IS, AND WHY IT IS *NOT* THE PI 3's RNG
;  ======================================================================
;
;  Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi:125-128
;
;      rng@7e104000 {
;          compatible = "brcm,bcm2711-rng200";
;          reg = <0x7e104000 0x28>;
;      };
;
;  Bus $7E104000 is ARM physical $FE104000 - the soc node's own ranges
;  property, Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi:41. That is the same window
;  every other peripheral in this library set is reached through, and
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/soc.pi4 carries the same address as #SOC_RNG_BASE
;  with the same citation.
;
;  THE PART NUMBER IN THAT compatible STRING IS THE WHOLE WARNING. The
;  Pi 3 and everything before it carry Broadcom's ORIGINAL RNG, driven
;  by Linux as bcm2835-rng. The Pi 4 carries an RNG200, driven as
;  iproc-rng200. THE TWO REGISTER MAPS ARE DIFFERENT AND THEY OVERLAP,
;  which is the dangerous kind of different:
;
;    offset  bcm2835 RNG (Pi 3)        RNG200 (Pi 4, this file)
;    ------  ------------------------  -------------------------------
;    $00     RNG_CTRL, enable = bit 0  RNG_CTRL, enable = bit 0
;    $04     RNG_STATUS. Word count    RNG_SOFT_RESET
;            in bits 31:24, AND the
;            warm-up count is WRITTEN
;            here
;    $08     RNG_DATA - the data!      RBG_SOFT_RESET
;    $10     RNG_INT_MASK              RNG_TOTAL_BIT_COUNT_THRESHOLD
;    $20     -                         RNG_FIFO_DATA
;    $24     -                         RNG_FIFO_COUNT
;
;  Both are compared here from files in this repository:
;  RaspberryPi4/Reference/v6.12_bcm2835-rng.c:17-26 for the left column,
;  RaspberryPi4/Reference/rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:21-48 for the right.
;
;  READ THE TABLE AGAIN AND NOTICE WHAT WOULD HAPPEN. A program written
;  against the Pi 3 map, running on a Pi 4, would take its data from
;  $08 - which on this part is RBG_SOFT_RESET - and would write its
;  warm-up count into $04, which on this part is RNG_SOFT_RESET. It
;  would not fault. $FE104000 is real, powered peripheral space, so
;  every one of those accesses returns or accepts a number. The output
;  would be a stream of whatever RBG_SOFT_RESET reads as, and it would
;  go straight into a seed. This is exactly the failure drbg.pi4's
;  header refused to risk, and it is why this file cites a line number
;  for every offset below rather than a chip family for all of them.
;
;  ======================================================================
;   THE THREE SOURCES, AND WHERE THEY DISAGREE
;  ======================================================================
;
;  Three drivers for this block are in RaspberryPi4/Reference/. All
;  three were fetched on 2026-08-28 because NOTHING under Datasheets/
;  documents the RNG200: bcm2711-peripherals.pdf, which is the
;  register-level authority for this chip, does not contain its
;  register map, and drbg.pi4's header records that search.
;
;    A. rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c   Raspberry Pi's own kernel fork.
;                                   THE AUTHORITY FOR THIS FILE.
;    B. v6.12_iproc-rng200.c        mainline Linux 6.12.
;    C. v2025.01_iproc_rng200.c     U-Boot v2025.01.
;
;  A, B and C AGREE, CHARACTER FOR CHARACTER, ON EVERY REGISTER OFFSET
;  AND EVERY BIT MASK they have in common. Three independent trees
;  carrying the same numbers is as close to a datasheet as this block
;  has here. B and C are register-map witnesses only; neither is
;  followed for behaviour, and the next paragraph is why.
;
;  THEY DO NOT AGREE ON HOW TO START IT, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE
;  WARM-UP. B and C enable the block by setting one bit and start
;  reading. A has a SEPARATE code path selected by name for this exact
;  part - `of_device_is_compatible(dev->of_node, "brcm,bcm2711-rng200")`
;  at rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:254-256, choosing bcm2711_rng200_init
;  and bcm2711_rng200_read instead of the generic pair - and that path
;  configures a warm-up and waits for it.
;
;  A FORK-SPECIFIC CODE PATH SELECTED BY THE PART NAME IS NOT AN
;  OPTIMISATION. Raspberry Pi added it for their own silicon; mainline
;  does not have it. This file follows A.
;
;  ======================================================================
;   THE WARM-UP. THIS IS THE PART THAT IS EASY TO SKIP AND EXPENSIVE.
;  ======================================================================
;
;  THE NUMBER IS $40000 - 262144 - AND IT IS A COUNT OF BITS, NOT OF
;  WORDS AND NOT OF SAMPLES. It is written to
;  RNG_TOTAL_BIT_COUNT_THRESHOLD at offset $10:
;
;      rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:212-214
;          /* initial numbers generated are "less random" so will be
;             discarded */
;          val = 0x40000;
;          iowrite32(val, priv->base +
;                    RNG_TOTAL_BIT_COUNT_THRESHOLD_OFFSET);
;
;  SO THE DISCARD IS DONE BY THE HARDWARE, NOT BY THE DRIVER, and that
;  is the single most important sentence in this file. There is no
;  "read N words and throw them away" loop anywhere in any of the three
;  drivers. The threshold is programmed once, before the block is
;  enabled, and the block itself withholds the early output. A port
;  that reproduces a software discard loop instead of writing $10 has
;  reproduced the comment and not the mechanism.
;
;  THE SOFTWARE SIDE OF IT IS A DIFFERENT NUMBER AND A DIFFERENT
;  REGISTER, and both are needed:
;
;      rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:176-182
;          /* ensure warm up period has elapsed */
;          while (1) {
;                  val = ioread32(priv->base +
;                                 RNG_TOTAL_BIT_COUNT_OFFSET);
;                  if (val > 16)
;                          break;
;                  cpu_relax();
;          }
;
;  RNG_TOTAL_BIT_COUNT is at $0C and is a read-only running count. The
;  driver waits for it to pass 16 before its FIRST read. EntropyBegin()
;  writes the threshold; EntropyWarmupWait() is that loop, bounded.
;
;  WHAT IS *NOT* CLAIMED HERE, because none of the three files says it:
;  the internal semantics of the threshold register. Whether the block
;  suppresses FIFO fills until TOTAL_BIT_COUNT reaches the threshold,
;  or raises the TOTAL_BITS_COUNT interrupt at that point and something
;  else does the suppressing, is not stated by any source on this disk.
;  What IS stated is the comment quoted above, in Raspberry Pi's own
;  driver, next to the write. This file reproduces the write, the value
;  and the ordering exactly, and does not paraphrase the mechanism.
;
;  THE $40000 IS A COINCIDENCE WORTH KNOWING ABOUT. bcm2835-rng.c:26
;  carries `#define RNG_WARMUP_COUNT 0x40000` with a nearly identical
;  comment - and writes it to RNG_STATUS at offset $04, which on THIS
;  part is RNG_SOFT_RESET. Same number, different register, different
;  block. Anybody who remembers "the Pi warm-up is 0x40000" and writes
;  it to the Pi 3's offset has just asked the RNG200 to soft-reset
;  itself on a schedule. The number carried over; the address did not.
;
;  ======================================================================
;   THE FIFO. NEVER READ IT BEFORE THE BLOCK SAYS IT HAS SOMETHING.
;  ======================================================================
;
;  RNG_FIFO_COUNT at $24, low eight bits, is how many 32-bit words are
;  waiting (rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:46-47). RNG_FIFO_DATA at $20 is
;  the data. Every read in this file is guarded:
;
;      rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:184-192
;          /* ensure fifo is not empty */
;          while (1) {
;                  num_words = ioread32(priv->base +
;                                       RNG_FIFO_COUNT_OFFSET) &
;                              RNG_FIFO_COUNT_RNG_FIFO_COUNT_MASK;
;                  if (num_words) break;
;                  ...
;
;  A READ OF AN EMPTY FIFO RETURNS SOMETHING. It is a register in a
;  live block; it returns a number, probably the last word, possibly
;  zero, and nothing in the hardware complains. That number then looks
;  exactly like entropy to everything downstream. There is no version
;  of this that fails loudly on its own, which is why the guard is not
;  optional and why EntropyWords() counts what it actually got instead
;  of trusting that it got what it asked for.
;
;  THE FIFO THRESHOLD at $24 bits 15:8 is written to 2 by the driver
;  (rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:215-217, "min fifo count to generate full
;  interrupt"). This file writes it too, for one reason only: to keep
;  the enable sequence byte-for-byte the same as the driver's. Nothing
;  here uses interrupts. Leaving one write out of a sequence that is
;  known to work, on the grounds that it looks unnecessary, is how a
;  reproduction stops being a reproduction.
;
;  ======================================================================
;   THE ENABLE WORD, AND THE TWO FIELDS IN IT
;  ======================================================================
;
;      rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:218-220
;          /* enable the rng - 1Mhz sample rate */
;          val = (0x3 << RNG_CTRL_RNG_DIV_CTRL_SHIFT) |
;                RNG_CTRL_RNG_RBGEN_MASK;
;          iowrite32(val, priv->base + RNG_CTRL_OFFSET);
;
;  RNG_CTRL_RNG_DIV_CTRL_SHIFT is 13 (:24) and RNG_CTRL_RNG_RBGEN_MASK
;  is $00001FFF (:22). So the word is $00007FFF: divider field 3, and
;  ALL THIRTEEN bits of the RBGEN field set.
;
;  NOTE THAT THE FORK SETS THE WHOLE THIRTEEN-BIT FIELD AND MAINLINE
;  SETS ONE BIT. Mainline's iproc_rng200_enable_set clears the mask and
;  ORs in RNG_CTRL_RNG_RBGEN_ENABLE = 1 (v6.12_iproc-rng200.c:52-58).
;  Raspberry Pi's bcm2711 path ORs in the whole mask. This file writes
;  $00007FFF because it follows the fork, and the difference is
;  recorded here rather than smoothed over: if a future silicon run
;  finds the block behaving oddly, "which of the two enable words did
;  we write" is a question somebody will need answered, and the answer
;  should not have to be reconstructed from a diff.
;
;  ======================================================================
;   THE ALREADY-ENABLED CASE, AND WHY IT GETS ITS OWN RETURN VALUE
;  ======================================================================
;
;      rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:209-210
;          if (ioread32(priv->base + RNG_CTRL_OFFSET) &
;              RNG_CTRL_RNG_RBGEN_MASK)
;                  return 0;
;
;  The driver leaves an already-running block alone. So does
;  EntropyBegin() - re-programming the threshold under a running block
;  would restart a warm-up that had already been paid for, and might do
;  worse.
;
;  BUT "SOMEBODY ELSE ENABLED IT" IS NOT THE SAME FACT AS "THE WARM-UP
;  WAS CONFIGURED", and this file will not let those two be confused.
;  U-Boot's driver enables this block by setting bit 0 and NEVER writes
;  the threshold at $10 (v2025.01_iproc_rng200.c:39-52, :140) - it has
;  no bcm2711 path at all. A Pi 4 that came up through a U-Boot which
;  touched the RNG therefore has a RUNNING block whose warm-up
;  threshold is whatever reset left there, and this file cannot know
;  what that is.
;
;  So EntropyBegin() returns #ENTROPY_ALREADY - a distinct, POSITIVE,
;  non-#ENTROPY_OK value - and the caller decides. A caller that wants
;  the documented warm-up regardless calls EntropyRestart(), which
;  performs the soft-reset sequence and then the full enable. The
;  alternative designs were both worse: silently re-initialising would
;  throw away a good warm-up, and silently accepting would hand back
;  words from a block that may never have discarded anything.
;
;  ======================================================================
;   HEALTH: THE TWO STATUS BITS THAT MEAN "DO NOT USE THIS OUTPUT"
;  ======================================================================
;
;  RNG_INT_STATUS at $18 carries (rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:36-40):
;
;      $80000000  MASTER_FAIL_LOCKOUT
;      $00020000  STARTUP_TRANSITIONS_MET
;      $00000020  NIST_FAIL
;      $00000001  TOTAL_BITS_COUNT
;
;  Mainline checks MASTER_FAIL_LOCKOUT | NIST_FAIL before every read
;  and restarts the block once if either is set
;  (v6.12_iproc-rng200.c:106-115). RASPBERRY PI'S bcm2711 READ PATH
;  DOES NOT - it checks neither.
;
;  THIS FILE KEEPS THE CHECK, and departs from its own authority to do
;  it. The reason is one-directional: a block that has declared its own
;  output failed is the single case where continuing produces bytes
;  that look perfect and are not. Skipping the check costs nothing
;  visible and can only ever lose; keeping it costs one register read
;  per word and can only ever refuse output that the hardware has
;  already condemned. Where two cited sources differ and one of them
;  refuses more, this project takes the refusal.
;
;  STARTUP_TRANSITIONS_MET IS NOT USED AS A GATE and that is
;  deliberate. It is named in the fork and in mainline, and NEITHER
;  driver ever tests it - mainline defines the mask and reads it in no
;  expression, the fork the same. Nothing on this disk says what
;  "transitions met" means, whether it latches, or whether it is set on
;  a part where the startup test is disabled. EntropyIntStatus()
;  exposes the whole register so a bench run can print it and find out;
;  it is not wired into a decision on the strength of its name.
;
;  ======================================================================
;   THE WAITS ARE COUNTED IN SPINS, NOT MICROSECONDS
;  ======================================================================
;
;  Every wait in this file takes a spin budget and gives up. Nothing
;  here can hang the board, and that is worth more than a wait
;  expressed in real time would be.
;
;  Using timer.pi4 would give real time and would cost a mandatory
;  dependency (and TimerInit() having been called) on a file whose
;  whole job is to be callable from a bring-up program before anything
;  else works. A spin count is not a duration - it depends on the core
;  clock, on the cache state and on whether the MMU is on - so DO NOT
;  read #ENTROPY_SPINS as a timeout in seconds. It is a bound that
;  makes a dead block report itself instead of freezing the machine.
;  #ENTROPY_SPINS is generous by construction; see its comment.
;
;  ======================================================================
;   EntropyStuckCheck - WHAT IT PROVES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
;  ======================================================================
;
;  READ THIS BEFORE QUOTING ITS RESULT ANYWHERE.
;
;  IT IS A STUCK-AT / DEAD-BLOCK DETECTOR. IT IS NOT A RANDOMNESS TEST
;  AND MUST NEVER BE DESCRIBED AS ONE.
;
;  WHAT IT ACTUALLY DOES, on a caller-supplied buffer of N 32-bit
;  words that EntropyWords() filled:
;
;    1. ALL WORDS IDENTICAL. Sets #ENTROPY_STUCK_ALLEQUAL.
;    2. SHORT REPEATING CYCLE. For each period p from 2 to
;       #ENTROPY_STUCK_MAXCYCLE, tests whether w[i] = w[i-p] for every
;       i. Any p that holds sets #ENTROPY_STUCK_CYCLE.
;    3. ONES/ZEROS BALANCE. Counts set bits over all 32N bits and
;       requires the count to lie within #ENTROPY_STUCK_BIAS_PPT parts
;       per thousand of half. Outside that, sets #ENTROPY_STUCK_BIAS.
;
;  It also records, for printing rather than for judging: the set-bit
;  count, the total bit count, the period it found if any, and how many
;  words were exactly $00000000 and exactly $FFFFFFFF.
;
;  WHAT A PASS ESTABLISHES: the block is running, the FIFO is
;  advancing, the data lines are not tied, the output is not one value
;  repeated, and it is not grossly biased toward ones or zeros. That
;  is the failure mode that actually happens - a block that was never
;  enabled, a base address off by a page, a read of a register that is
;  not the FIFO - and every one of those produces output that a casual
;  eye reads as fine.
;
;  WHAT A PASS DOES NOT ESTABLISH, and this list is not padding:
;
;    * NOT that the output is random. A 32-bit counter passes all
;      three tests. So does the low half of any decent PRNG. So does
;      AES in counter mode under a key an attacker chose.
;    * NOT that the output is unpredictable. Nothing here looks for
;      structure beyond period 64.
;    * NOT an entropy rate. It cannot distinguish 32 bits of entropy
;      per word from 1.
;    * NOT NIST SP 800-90B health testing. There is no repetition-count
;      test and no adaptive-proportion test here, and the block's own
;      NIST_FAIL bit - which IS such a test, in hardware - is checked
;      separately and is not part of this verdict.
;    * NOT a statement about any word other than the ones in the
;      buffer. It is a sample, taken once.
;
;  THE BIAS BAND IS A DEAD-BLOCK THRESHOLD, NOT A STATISTICAL TEST.
;  #ENTROPY_STUCK_BIAS_PPT is 10, so a run is refused only if the set-
;  bit fraction leaves 49.0%..51.0%. At the recommended 4096 words that
;  is 131072 bits, where the standard deviation of the set-bit count
;  for a fair source is 181 bits, and the band is +/- 1310 - about
;  seven standard deviations. IT IS CHOSEN TO NEVER FIRE ON A HEALTHY
;  SOURCE, because a self-test that cries wolf gets disabled. It will
;  therefore MISS a source that is merely bad. That is the correct
;  trade for this instrument and it is the reason the list above says
;  what it says.
;
;  THERE IS NO ORACLE FOR THIS. Every other gate in this tree checks an
;  implementation against an independent one - NIST vectors, a Python
;  model, a second driver. A hardware entropy source has no expected
;  output by definition, so there is nothing to compare against and no
;  gate can ever say the numbers were right. What CAN be gated is this
;  detector's own logic, and tools/a64/a64_entropy_check.py does that
;  by feeding it constructed streams whose verdict is known. Say that
;  plainly wherever the result is reported.
;
;  ======================================================================
;   WHAT HAS BEEN PROVEN - 2026-08-28
;  ======================================================================
;
;  PROVEN, under the project's independent A64 model
;  (tools/a64/a64_interp.py) with the RNG200 registers emulated by
;  tools/a64/a64_entropy_check.py, which is NOT hardware:
;
;    * The enable sequence writes exactly the four words the driver
;      writes, to exactly the four offsets, IN THE DRIVER'S ORDER, and
;      writes nothing else. The gate records every MMIO access and
;      compares the transcript.
;    * EntropyBegin() on an already-enabled block writes NOTHING and
;      returns #ENTROPY_ALREADY.
;    * EntropyRestart() reproduces the soft-reset transcript.
;    * No FIFO read is ever issued while the modelled FIFO_COUNT is 0.
;      The gate faults the run if one is.
;    * The warm-up wait does not return before TOTAL_BIT_COUNT passes
;      16, and gives up rather than spinning for ever.
;    * MASTER_FAIL_LOCKOUT and NIST_FAIL each stop the read.
;    * The stuck detector's verdict on eight constructed streams -
;      constant, alternating, period 7, period 64, period 65 (which it
;      must NOT catch, and the gate asserts it does not), all-ones,
;      all-zeros, and a counter (which it must NOT catch either).
;
;  NOT PROVEN:
;    * NOTHING HERE HAS RUN ON SILICON. Not one register of the real
;      block has been read. Every offset is a citation, not a
;      measurement.
;    * NO STATEMENT ABOUT THE QUALITY OF THE HARDWARE'S OUTPUT, of any
;      kind, has been made or can be made here.
;    * The spin budgets have never been measured against a real block's
;      latency. #ENTROPY_SPINS is generous, not calibrated.
; ======================================================================

EnableExplicit

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE BASE. Kept as a literal here rather than taken from soc.pi4's
;  #SOC_RNG_BASE, for the same reason uart.pi4:476-488 keeps its two:
;  a bring-up program that wants entropy should not be forced to define
;  #SOC_TARGET and include soc.pi4 as well.
;
;  THE DUPLICATION IS MACHINE-CHECKED, NOT TRUSTED.
;  tools/a64/a64_entropy_check.py reads #SOC_RNG_BASE out of soc.pi4
;  and FAILS if this line disagrees with it. (uart.pi4 claims the same
;  arrangement for its two bases and names a gate,
;  tools/a64/a64_socbase_check.py, that is not in the tree - so that
;  pair really is trusted rather than checked. Reported, not fixed
;  here; uart.pi4 is not this task's file.)
;
;  bcm2711.dtsi:125 rng@7e104000, translated by bcm2711.dtsi:41.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ENTROPY_BASE = $FE104000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  RNG200 REGISTER OFFSETS AND BIT MASKS.
;
;  Every line cites RaspberryPi4/Reference/rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c,
;  which is Raspberry Pi's own kernel fork and the only one of the
;  three drivers with a code path written for this part by name. The
;  mainline and U-Boot copies in the same folder agree on all of them.
;
;  32-BIT REGISTERS, PeekN / PokeN ONLY. A bare Peek would be a 64-bit
;  access - two registers at once on a device aperture, which is a
;  fatal unaligned-width access with the MMU off and is not a thing
;  device space is required to service at all. PeekN and not PeekL
;  because these are unsigned patterns: MASTER_FAIL_LOCKOUT is bit 31,
;  and PeekL sign-extends since 2026-08-25, which would make
;  `status & $80000000` fire on a sign extension rather than on the
;  bit. That is the whole reason .n exists.
;
;  The dtsi says the block is $28 bytes long (bcm2711.dtsi:127) and the
;  highest offset below is $24, four bytes wide. The two agree exactly,
;  which is a cheap independent check that this map is the map for this
;  node and not for some other RNG200 instance.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#RNG_CTRL_OFF           = $00        ; :21
#RNG_CTRL_RBGEN_MASK    = $00001FFF  ; :22
#RNG_CTRL_RBGEN_ENABLE  = $00000001  ; :23
#RNG_CTRL_DIV_SHIFT     = 13         ; :24  RNG_CTRL_RNG_DIV_CTRL_SHIFT

#RNG_SOFT_RESET_OFF     = $04        ; :26
#RNG_SOFT_RESET_BIT     = $00000001  ; :27

#RBG_SOFT_RESET_OFF     = $08        ; :29
#RBG_SOFT_RESET_BIT     = $00000001  ; :30

#RNG_TOTAL_BITS_OFF     = $0C        ; :32  RNG_TOTAL_BIT_COUNT
#RNG_TOTAL_BITS_THR_OFF = $10        ; :34  ..._THRESHOLD

#RNG_INT_STATUS_OFF     = $18        ; :36
#RNG_INT_MASTER_FAIL    = $80000000  ; :37  MASTER_FAIL_LOCKOUT
#RNG_INT_STARTUP_MET    = $00020000  ; :38  STARTUP_TRANSITIONS_MET
#RNG_INT_NIST_FAIL      = $00000020  ; :39  NIST_FAIL
#RNG_INT_TOTAL_BITS     = $00000001  ; :40  TOTAL_BITS_COUNT
#RNG_INT_CLEAR_ALL      = $FFFFFFFF  ; the driver's restart writes this

#RNG_INT_ENABLE_OFF     = $1C        ; :42  named, never written here

#RNG_FIFO_DATA_OFF      = $20        ; :44
#RNG_FIFO_COUNT_OFF     = $24        ; :46
#RNG_FIFO_COUNT_MASK    = $000000FF  ; :47
#RNG_FIFO_THR_SHIFT     = 8          ; :48  RNG_FIFO_THRESHOLD_SHIFT

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE THREE VALUES THE bcm2711 PATH PROGRAMS.
;
;  Spelled as the finished words rather than as shifts, because a
;  constant declaration in this language may not contain arithmetic
;  (soc.pi4's header records the refusal). The derivation is written
;  beside each one so the literal can be checked by eye, and
;  a64_entropy_check.py re-derives all three from the shifts above and
;  fails if they disagree.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

;  $40000 = 262144 bits. rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:213.
#ENTROPY_WARMUP_BITS = $00040000

;  2 << #RNG_FIFO_THR_SHIFT  =  2 << 8  =  $200.  :216-217.
#ENTROPY_FIFO_THR_WORD = $00000200

;  (3 << #RNG_CTRL_DIV_SHIFT) | #RNG_CTRL_RBGEN_MASK
;    = (3 << 13) | $1FFF  =  $6000 | $1FFF  =  $7FFF.  :219.
#ENTROPY_ENABLE_WORD = $00007FFF

;  The driver's own software-side warm-up gate: TOTAL_BIT_COUNT > 16.
;  :178-180.
#ENTROPY_READY_BITS = 16

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  RETURN CODES. Every refusal is negative and every success is
;  positive, so one comparison separates them - the same rule
;  drbg.pi4 states for itself.
;
;  #ENTROPY_ALREADY is POSITIVE because the block IS usable; it is a
;  fact about provenance, not a failure. A caller that does not care
;  writes `If st < 0`; a caller that does care tests for it by name.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ENTROPY_OK          =  1
#ENTROPY_ALREADY     =  2
#ENTROPY_ERR_TIMEOUT = -1   ; a bounded wait ran out of spins
#ENTROPY_ERR_HEALTH  = -2   ; MASTER_FAIL_LOCKOUT or NIST_FAIL is set
#ENTROPY_ERR_OFF     = -3   ; the block is not enabled
#ENTROPY_ERR_ARG     = -4   ; a null pointer or a non-positive count

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE DEFAULT SPIN BUDGET. NOT A DURATION - see the header.
;
;  Four million iterations of a loop whose body is one device read. On
;  a 1.5 GHz Cortex-A72 with the MMU off, a Device-nGnRnE load is not
;  cached and not merged, so this is somewhere between a fraction of a
;  second and several seconds. The block is documented as sampling at
;  1 MHz (rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:218), so one 32-bit FIFO word is
;  about 32 microseconds of sampling and the whole $40000-bit warm-up
;  is about a quarter of a second. The budget has to clear that with
;  room, and it does.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ENTROPY_SPINS = 4000000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  STUCK-DETECTOR VERDICT BITS. Zero means every check passed.
;  A verdict is a BITMASK, not a code: two failures are two bits.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ENTROPY_STUCK_OK       = 0
#ENTROPY_STUCK_ALLEQUAL = 1   ; every word identical
#ENTROPY_STUCK_CYCLE    = 2   ; repeats with a period of 2..64
#ENTROPY_STUCK_BIAS     = 4   ; set-bit fraction outside 49.0%..51.0%
#ENTROPY_STUCK_SHORT    = 8   ; fewer than two words to look at

;  The longest period the cycle test looks for. 64 words is 2048 bits,
;  which is far more state than any plausible stuck-at or wiring
;  failure carries, and the test costs 64 passes over the buffer.
#ENTROPY_STUCK_MAXCYCLE = 64

;  The bias band, in parts per thousand either side of half. See the
;  header: this is a dead-block threshold, deliberately far too wide
;  to be a statistical test.
#ENTROPY_STUCK_BIAS_PPT = 10

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MEASUREMENTS FROM THE LAST EntropyStuckCheck, for printing.
;
;  Globals, so they are zero at reset (the __a64_bss_zero loop in the
;  standard prologue). They are OUTPUTS OF THE LAST RUN and mean
;  nothing before the first one; EntropyStuckWords() is 0 until then,
;  which is how a caller tells.
;
;  They exist because a verdict of "failed" that cannot say WHAT it saw
;  sends the next person back to the board to find out. Printing the
;  set-bit count next to the total is one line and answers most of it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global entStuckWords.i        ; how many words the last check looked at
Global entStuckOnes.i         ; set bits counted across them
Global entStuckBits.i         ; total bits looked at (words * 32)
Global entStuckPeriod.i       ; the repeating period found, else 0
Global entStuckZeroWords.i    ; words that were exactly $00000000
Global entStuckOnesWords.i    ; words that were exactly $FFFFFFFF

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Counters that survive across calls, for a bring-up print.
;  entTimeouts is the one worth watching: a board that produces
;  entropy but times out sometimes is a different problem from a board
;  that produces none.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global entWordsRead.i         ; FIFO words this program has consumed
Global entTimeouts.i          ; bounded waits that ran out
Global entHealthStops.i       ; reads stopped by a health bit

; ======================================================================
;  THE REGISTER LAYER
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EntropyBarrier() - dsb sy + isb, from
;  RaspberryPi4/Intrinsics/bcm2711_hardware.def.
;
;  Exposed as a procedure for the same reason GpioBarrier() is: so a
;  caller sequencing this block against another peripheral has one
;  place to reach for, and so there is one line to change if the
;  barrier ever needs to be weaker.
;
;  WHY THERE IS ONE AROUND EVERY ACCESS HERE AND NOT ONLY AT THE EDGES.
;  This block is not like GPIO, where a burst of writes to one
;  peripheral can share a barrier. Every read of RNG_FIFO_DATA has a
;  SIDE EFFECT - it consumes a word - and its result depends on a
;  RNG_FIFO_COUNT read that must have completed first. Letting those
;  two be reordered or merged is precisely the way to read an empty
;  FIFO while believing the guard held.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure EntropyBarrier()
  a64_barrier()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EntropyReg(off) / EntropySetReg(off, v) - one 32-bit register.
;
;  INTERNAL. The public accessors below name the registers; these two
;  exist so the barrier discipline lives in one place instead of at
;  thirty call sites.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i EntropyReg(off.i)
  Protected v.i
  EntropyBarrier()
  v = PeekN(#ENTROPY_BASE + off)
  EntropyBarrier()
  ProcedureReturn v & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

Procedure EntropySetReg(off.i, v.i)
  EntropyBarrier()
  PokeN(#ENTROPY_BASE + off, v & $FFFFFFFF)
  EntropyBarrier()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The named readers. All four are public because a bring-up program
;  that can print these four numbers can diagnose this block without
;  another build.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i EntropyCtrl()
  ProcedureReturn EntropyReg(#RNG_CTRL_OFF)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i EntropyIntStatus()
  ProcedureReturn EntropyReg(#RNG_INT_STATUS_OFF)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i EntropyTotalBits()
  ProcedureReturn EntropyReg(#RNG_TOTAL_BITS_OFF)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i EntropyFifoCount()
  ProcedureReturn EntropyReg(#RNG_FIFO_COUNT_OFF) & #RNG_FIFO_COUNT_MASK
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EntropyEnabled() - 1 if any bit of the RBGEN field is set.
;
;  THE TEST IS THE MASK, NOT BIT 0, because the fork's enable word sets
;  all thirteen and mainline's sets one. Testing bit 0 alone would
;  still be right for both today - bit 0 is in both words - but the
;  driver's own already-enabled test is written against the mask
;  (rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:209) and this reproduces it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i EntropyEnabled()
  If (EntropyCtrl() & #RNG_CTRL_RBGEN_MASK) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EntropyHealthy() - 0 if the block has condemned its own output.
;
;  MASTER_FAIL_LOCKOUT or NIST_FAIL. See the header for why this check
;  is kept when Raspberry Pi's own bcm2711 read path drops it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i EntropyHealthy()
  Protected s.i
  s = EntropyIntStatus()
  If (s & #RNG_INT_MASTER_FAIL) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (s & #RNG_INT_NIST_FAIL) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STARTING THE BLOCK
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  entropy_Program() - the four writes, in the driver's order.
;
;  INTERNAL, and the order is the point. The threshold and the FIFO
;  threshold are written BEFORE the enable word, exactly as
;  rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:212-220 writes them. Programming a warm-up
;  threshold into a block that is already generating would be asking a
;  different question, and it is not the question the driver asks.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure entropy_Program()
  EntropySetReg(#RNG_TOTAL_BITS_THR_OFF, #ENTROPY_WARMUP_BITS)
  EntropySetReg(#RNG_FIFO_COUNT_OFF, #ENTROPY_FIFO_THR_WORD)
  EntropySetReg(#RNG_CTRL_OFF, #ENTROPY_ENABLE_WORD)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EntropyBegin() - enable the block if nobody else has.
;
;  Returns #ENTROPY_OK if this call programmed and enabled it, or
;  #ENTROPY_ALREADY if it was already running and was left alone. Both
;  are positive; see the header for why the distinction is handed back
;  rather than swallowed.
;
;  IT CANNOT FAIL, because nothing it does can fail: three writes to
;  registers that are always there. Failures show up at the first read,
;  which is where they can be attributed.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i EntropyBegin()
  If EntropyEnabled() = 1
    ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_ALREADY
  EndIf
  entropy_Program()
  ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_OK
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EntropyRestart() - disable, clear, soft-reset both cores, re-enable.
;
;  The sequence is iproc_rng200_restart, v6.12_iproc-rng200.c:61-88,
;  which U-Boot carries identically at v2025.01_iproc_rng200.c:54-82.
;  Two independent trees, same eight accesses, same order:
;
;      disable (clear the RBGEN field in RNG_CTRL)
;      write $FFFFFFFF to RNG_INT_STATUS          - clear all status
;      set   RBG_SOFT_RESET bit in $08
;      set   RNG_SOFT_RESET bit in $04
;      clear RNG_SOFT_RESET bit in $04
;      clear RBG_SOFT_RESET bit in $08
;      enable
;
;  THE ORDER IS ASYMMETRIC AND THAT IS NOT A TYPO IN THE DRIVER. RBG is
;  asserted first and released last; RNG is asserted second and
;  released first. Both copies do it that way. It is reproduced, not
;  tidied - a reset sequence is exactly the kind of thing where the
;  order is the whole content and nobody remembers why.
;
;  WHAT IS DIFFERENT HERE: this file's last step is the FULL bcm2711
;  enable - the warm-up threshold, the FIFO threshold, then $7FFF -
;  where the two drivers' restart re-enables with their own generic
;  one-bit write. Restarting is precisely the moment the warm-up has
;  to be re-established, and a restart that left the threshold to
;  whatever it was is the silent half of the bug this file exists to
;  avoid.
;
;  Returns #ENTROPY_OK. Like EntropyBegin() it cannot fail; the first
;  read is where a dead block reports itself.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i EntropyRestart()
  Protected v.i

  ; disable: clear the whole RBGEN field, keep everything else
  v = EntropyCtrl()
  v = v & ~#RNG_CTRL_RBGEN_MASK
  EntropySetReg(#RNG_CTRL_OFF, v)

  EntropySetReg(#RNG_INT_STATUS_OFF, #RNG_INT_CLEAR_ALL)

  v = EntropyReg(#RBG_SOFT_RESET_OFF)
  EntropySetReg(#RBG_SOFT_RESET_OFF, v | #RBG_SOFT_RESET_BIT)

  v = EntropyReg(#RNG_SOFT_RESET_OFF)
  EntropySetReg(#RNG_SOFT_RESET_OFF, v | #RNG_SOFT_RESET_BIT)

  v = EntropyReg(#RNG_SOFT_RESET_OFF)
  EntropySetReg(#RNG_SOFT_RESET_OFF, v & ~#RNG_SOFT_RESET_BIT)

  v = EntropyReg(#RBG_SOFT_RESET_OFF)
  EntropySetReg(#RBG_SOFT_RESET_OFF, v & ~#RBG_SOFT_RESET_BIT)

  entropy_Program()
  ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_OK
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EntropyStop() - clear the RBGEN field and stop generating.
;
;  iproc_rng200_cleanup, v6.12_iproc-rng200.c:160-165. Present so that
;  a diagnostic can put the block back the way it found it, and so
;  that a program which has finished with entropy is not leaving a
;  noise source running. It does NOT reset anything: a later
;  EntropyBegin() will see the block disabled and program the warm-up
;  again, which is the correct restart.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure EntropyStop()
  Protected v.i
  v = EntropyCtrl()
  v = v & ~#RNG_CTRL_RBGEN_MASK
  EntropySetReg(#RNG_CTRL_OFF, v)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EntropyWarmupWait(spins) - block until TOTAL_BIT_COUNT passes 16.
;
;  rpi-6.12.y_iproc-rng200.c:176-182, bounded. Returns #ENTROPY_OK,
;  #ENTROPY_ERR_TIMEOUT, or #ENTROPY_ERR_OFF if the block is not even
;  enabled - because waiting for a counter to move in a block that is
;  switched off is the wait that would otherwise burn the whole budget
;  and then report a timeout, which is a true answer to the wrong
;  question.
;
;  THE DRIVER'S LOOP IS UNBOUNDED. This one is not, and the difference
;  is the whole argument for the spin budget: the kernel has a
;  watchdog, a console and a scheduler. A bare-metal image at $200000
;  with no exception vector has none of those, and an unbounded wait
;  on a dead peripheral is indistinguishable from a hang.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i EntropyWarmupWait(spins.i)
  Protected left.i

  If EntropyEnabled() = 0
    ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_ERR_OFF
  EndIf

  left = spins
  While left > 0
    If EntropyTotalBits() > #ENTROPY_READY_BITS
      ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_OK
    EndIf
    left = left - 1
  Wend
  entTimeouts = entTimeouts + 1
  ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_ERR_TIMEOUT
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EntropyTryWord(dst) - one nonblocking RNG200 service attempt.
;
;  Returns #ENTROPY_OK after writing exactly one 32-bit FIFO word, zero
;  while warm-up/FIFO production is still pending, or the same negative
;  OFF/HEALTH/ARG refusals as EntropyWords. There is NO retry loop here:
;  a cooperative owner supplies its own wrap-safe elapsed-time deadline.
;
;  The guards are the synchronous reader's guards, in explicit order: the
;  block must be enabled, the health bits must be clean, warm-up
;  TOTAL_BIT_COUNT must have passed 16, and FIFO_COUNT must say a word exists before
;  RNG_FIFO_DATA is touched. One call consumes at most one word.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i EntropyTryWord(dst.i)
  If dst = 0
    ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_ERR_ARG
  EndIf
  If EntropyEnabled() = 0
    ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_ERR_OFF
  EndIf
  If EntropyHealthy() = 0
    entHealthStops = entHealthStops + 1
    ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_ERR_HEALTH
  EndIf
  If EntropyTotalBits() <= #ENTROPY_READY_BITS
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If EntropyFifoCount() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  PokeN(dst, EntropyReg(#RNG_FIFO_DATA_OFF))
  entWordsRead = entWordsRead + 1
  ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_OK
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  READING
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EntropyWords(dst, count, spins) - count 32-bit words into dst.
;
;  Returns the number of words WRITTEN, which is `count` on success, or
;  a negative refusal having written however many it managed. A caller
;  that ignores the return value and uses the buffer anyway is using
;  whatever was in it, which is a bug it can find - the same argument
;  DrbgGenerate() makes for writing nothing at all when it refuses.
;
;  THE LOOP IS: check health, wait for FIFO_COUNT > 0 within the
;  budget, read one word, repeat. It does NOT read the count and then
;  drain that many words in a burst the way the driver does, and that
;  is deliberate: the burst form has to trust a count it read earlier,
;  and re-reading the count per word costs one device access against a
;  block that is producing about one word every thirty microseconds
;  anyway. Correctness first where the cost is invisible.
;
;  ONE RESTART, AT MOST, AND NOT HERE. Mainline restarts the block once
;  per read call when it sees a health bit
;  (v6.12_iproc-rng200.c:110-114). This file refuses instead and leaves
;  the restart to the caller, because a restart discards the warm-up
;  and a library that silently re-warms in the middle of filling a seed
;  buffer would produce a buffer whose two halves came from two
;  differently-conditioned runs. EntropyRestart() is public; the caller
;  can retry, and can say so.
;
;  ALIGNMENT: dst must be 4-byte aligned. PokeN of an unaligned address
;  is a fatal access with the MMU off - the rule
;  tools/a64/a64_interp.py has held since 2026-08-28 - and this file
;  does not check it, exactly as string.pi4 does not check its
;  destinations. A .a array or any Global is aligned.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i EntropyWords(dst.i, count.i, spins.i)
  Protected got.i
  Protected left.i
  Protected ready.i

  If dst = 0
    ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_ERR_ARG
  EndIf
  If count <= 0
    ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_ERR_ARG
  EndIf
  If EntropyEnabled() = 0
    ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_ERR_OFF
  EndIf

  got = 0
  While got < count
    If EntropyHealthy() = 0
      entHealthStops = entHealthStops + 1
      ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_ERR_HEALTH
    EndIf

    ; wait for at least one word, bounded
    ready = 0
    left = spins
    While left > 0
      If EntropyFifoCount() > 0
        ready = 1
        left = 0
      Else
        left = left - 1
      EndIf
    Wend
    If ready = 0
      entTimeouts = entTimeouts + 1
      ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_ERR_TIMEOUT
    EndIf

    PokeN(dst + (got * 4), EntropyReg(#RNG_FIFO_DATA_OFF))
    got = got + 1
    entWordsRead = entWordsRead + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn got
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EntropyBytes(dst, nbytes, spins) - nbytes of entropy into dst.
;
;  Returns nbytes on success or a negative refusal. Any number of bytes
;  is allowed; a partial final word is taken from the low end of one
;  FIFO word and THE REST OF THAT WORD IS DISCARDED.
;
;  DISCARDING IT IS THE POINT. Keeping the leftover for the next call
;  would mean the library held entropy in a global between calls, which
;  is state an attacker's fault-injection story can reach and which
;  makes two calls correlated in a way neither caller asked for. The
;  cost of throwing it away is at most three bytes per call from a
;  block that makes about a megabit a second.
;
;  BYTE ORDER: little-endian within the word - byte 0 of the output is
;  bits 7:0 of the FIFO word. That is the natural order on this part
;  and it is the order the driver's memcpy produces
;  (v6.12_iproc-rng200.c:129-132). It does not matter for entropy, and
;  it is written down because "it does not matter" is exactly the sort
;  of claim that later has to be checked against a wire format.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i EntropyBytes(dst.i, nbytes.i, spins.i)
  Protected done.i
  Protected w.i
  Protected r.i
  Protected k.i
  Protected st.i
  Protected wordbuf.i

  If dst = 0
    ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_ERR_ARG
  EndIf
  If nbytes <= 0
    ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_ERR_ARG
  EndIf

  done = 0
  While done < nbytes
    wordbuf = 0
    st = EntropyWords(@wordbuf, 1, spins)
    If st < 0
      ProcedureReturn st
    EndIf
    w = PeekN(@wordbuf)
    r = nbytes - done
    If r > 4
      r = 4
    EndIf
    k = 0
    While k < r
      PokeB(dst + done + k, (w >> (k * 8)) & 255)
      k = k + 1
    Wend
    done = done + r
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn done
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE STUCK-AT / DEAD-BLOCK DETECTOR
; ======================================================================
;
;  Read the header section of the same name before quoting any result
;  from this. It is not a randomness test.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  entropy_Popcount32(v) - set bits in the low 32 of v.
;
;  INTERNAL. A plain shift-and-test loop rather than the SWAR trick or
;  the A64 `cnt` instruction, because 32 iterations over a few thousand
;  words is nothing on an A72 and this file will not carry a clever
;  bit-twiddle that nothing in the tree can check independently.
;  bits.pi4 has a popcount; using it would make bits.pi4 a mandatory
;  include for anybody who wants entropy, which is the trade uart.pi4
;  declines for its bases and this file declines here.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i entropy_Popcount32(v.i)
  Protected n.i
  Protected x.i
  x = v & $FFFFFFFF
  n = 0
  While x <> 0
    n = n + (x & 1)
    x = x >> 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn n
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EntropyStuckCheck(buf, words) - the verdict, as a bitmask.
;
;  buf is a caller-owned array of `words` 32-bit words, already filled
;  by EntropyWords(). This routine performs NO hardware access at all -
;  which is what lets tools/a64/a64_entropy_check.py drive it with
;  constructed streams and know the answer in advance.
;
;  Returns #ENTROPY_STUCK_OK (0) or the OR of the failure bits, and
;  leaves its measurements in the entStuck* globals for printing.
;
;  A run of fewer than two words cannot say anything, and says so with
;  #ENTROPY_STUCK_SHORT rather than passing vacuously. A gate that
;  reports success on an empty sample is the shape of test this project
;  keeps a whole vault note about.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i EntropyStuckCheck(buf.i, words.i)
  Protected verdict.i
  Protected i.i
  Protected p.i
  Protected same.i
  Protected w.i
  Protected ones.i
  Protected bits.i
  Protected half.i
  Protected band.i
  Protected diff.i

  entStuckWords    = 0
  entStuckOnes     = 0
  entStuckBits     = 0
  entStuckPeriod   = 0
  entStuckZeroWords = 0
  entStuckOnesWords = 0

  If buf = 0
    ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_STUCK_SHORT
  EndIf
  If words < 2
    ProcedureReturn #ENTROPY_STUCK_SHORT
  EndIf

  verdict = #ENTROPY_STUCK_OK
  entStuckWords = words

  ; --- the ones count, and the two degenerate word values -----------
  ones = 0
  i = 0
  While i < words
    w = PeekN(buf + (i * 4))
    ones = ones + entropy_Popcount32(w)
    If w = 0
      entStuckZeroWords = entStuckZeroWords + 1
    EndIf
    If w = $FFFFFFFF
      entStuckOnesWords = entStuckOnesWords + 1
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  bits = words * 32
  entStuckOnes = ones
  entStuckBits = bits

  ; --- period 1: every word identical -------------------------------
  ;  Tested separately from the cycle loop so the verdict can say WHICH
  ;  of the two it was. A stuck data bus and a short shift-register
  ;  cycle are different repairs.
  same = 1
  i = 1
  While i < words
    If PeekN(buf + (i * 4)) <> PeekN(buf)
      same = 0
      i = words
    Else
      i = i + 1
    EndIf
  Wend
  If same = 1
    verdict = verdict | #ENTROPY_STUCK_ALLEQUAL
    entStuckPeriod = 1
  EndIf

  ; --- periods 2..#ENTROPY_STUCK_MAXCYCLE ---------------------------
  ;  A period p is only meaningful if the buffer holds more than p
  ;  words - "w[i] = w[i-p] for every i" is vacuously true when there
  ;  is no such i, and reporting a period the sample cannot support
  ;  would be the vacuous-pass failure again, in the other direction.
  If entStuckPeriod = 0
    p = 2
    While p <= #ENTROPY_STUCK_MAXCYCLE
      If words > p
        same = 1
        i = p
        While i < words
          If PeekN(buf + (i * 4)) <> PeekN(buf + ((i - p) * 4))
            same = 0
            i = words
          Else
            i = i + 1
          EndIf
        Wend
        If same = 1
          verdict = verdict | #ENTROPY_STUCK_CYCLE
          entStuckPeriod = p
          p = #ENTROPY_STUCK_MAXCYCLE
        EndIf
      EndIf
      p = p + 1
    Wend
  EndIf

  ; --- the balance band ---------------------------------------------
  ;  half = bits/2, band = bits * PPT / 1000 / 2... written out as
  ;  (bits / 1000) * PPT / 2 would lose precision on small samples, so
  ;  it is (bits * PPT) / 2000 with the multiply first. bits is at most
  ;  a few hundred thousand here and .i is 64 bits, so the product
  ;  cannot overflow.
  half = bits / 2
  band = (bits * #ENTROPY_STUCK_BIAS_PPT) / 2000
  diff = ones - half
  If diff < 0
    diff = 0 - diff
  EndIf
  If diff > band
    verdict = verdict | #ENTROPY_STUCK_BIAS
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn verdict
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The measurements from the last EntropyStuckCheck, readable.
;
;  Print these next to the verdict. A failure that says only "failed"
;  costs somebody a trip to the board; "127 words, period 7" does not.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i EntropyStuckWords()
  ProcedureReturn entStuckWords
EndProcedure

